{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HijXTcNJ5fN8qUFNc1yZZP","type":"tool_result","content":"`assemble` throws on zero or several markers and replaces via a\ncallback so `$&` stays literal. `server/app.ts` exports `createApp`\nwith an injectable `loadScaffold` and must stay free of Node imports.\nNothing may write to `index.html` (the old ssr-to-file scripts are\ngone). `npm run test:hydration` drives the app via `app.request()`.\nRoutes live in `server/app.ts`; bodies in `server/routes.ts`. Unknown\npaths go through Hono's `notFound` handler, which also assembles the\nscaffold. `client/index.ts` runs on every route, so it must null-check\nany element that only one route renders. `ssr:hydration` pipes into\n`node -` so a trailing route arg (`-- /list`) reaches the program.\n\nThe `html` templater hands components the original string for declared\nprops (`_Escaped._raw`), so a prop value is escaped once, by the\n\nSee [`builder.render`](../README.md#builderrenderfn-options) for the\nfull reference.\n\n### Self-closing tags\n\nInside `html` a component can be written as `<${HydraCounter} start=${5} />`,\nwhich is what `server/routes.ts` and `client/index.ts` do. That is fine\nbecause `html` always emits a real closing tag in the output. It is only\nin hand-written HTML that a custom element must not be self-closed, since\nthe browser would parse everything after it as its children.\n\n50:  routes over HTTP.\n51:- `npm run typecheck:hydration` -- type-check `server/` with Node types.\n60:The routes are `/example-hydration/` (a counter with a nested badge),\n97:`server/` runs only in Node:\n99:- `app.ts` -- the Hono app and its routes. It has no Node imports and\n101:- `routes.ts` -- the route bodies, written with `html`.\n212:which is what `server/routes.ts` and `client/index.ts` do. That is fine","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88e9b129-578a-4f21-9b36-91ad159ff531","uuid":"7aa344b9-274c-4a42-a335-9844d4